>
> Although the common belief is that player XL doesn't matter in the 
> end game, I'm not sure that's quite right.  (Watch out, source diving 
> ahead...)
>
> The way that the rndmonst function works is to determine a minimum
> and maximum monster difficulty, and then builds a table of eligible
> monsters, weighted by each monster's probability.  Then, whenever
> a monster is generated, one is picked out of that table at random.
> Therefore, the probability of any particular monster being generated 
> depends on how many other monsters are eligible for generation.
>
> The minimum and maximum monster levels are calculated as follows:
>
> level_difficulty = Depth of sanctum + (player XL / 2) if in the Planes
>   (zlevel)         Depth of sanctum if not in the Planes and carrying the
>                      Amulet of Yendor
>                    Current dungeon depth otherwise.
>
> minimum monster difficulty = level_difficulty / 6
> maximum monster difficulty = (level_difficulty + player XL) / 2
>
> Here's a table of what the monster difficulties look like for
> different combinations of sanctum dungeon level and player XL
> during the endgame (so in the dungeon while you have the amulet,
> and on the planes):
>
> Sanctum      zlevel   Difficulty   zlevel  Difficulty
>  DLVL   XL  (dungeon)  Min  Max   (planes)  Min Max
>  45     14    45        7   26      52      8   33
>  45     30    45        7   30      60      10  45
>  53     14    53        8   33      60      10  37
>  53     30    53        8   41      68      11  49
>
> The highest difficulty randomly-generated monsters are arch-liches, which
> have difficulty 29.  Archons are next, with difficulty 26.  So you can see
> from the table that the maxmimum difficulty will always be high enough to
> generate Archons.  It's possible to keep your XL low enough that arch-liches
> are never randomly generated as long as the Sanctum is shallow (although
> arch-liches are only randomly generated in Gehennom, and I would guess that 
> most arch-liches appear as a result of summoning rather than random 
> generation.)
>
> Where your XL comes into play is on the planes, where if your XL is low,
> then the minimum monster difficulty will be lower than if your XL is high.
> If there are more low-level monsters eligible for generation, that means
> that it's less likely that high-level monsters will be generated.  If your
> Sanctum was at dlvl 45, then if you keep your XL at level 14, everything
> with difficulty 8 or 9 is available for generation (and there's quite
> a few; there's a sorted table at 
> http://nethackwiki.com/wiki/Monster_difficulty).
>
> So: it is certainly true to keeping your XL artificially low will not
> prevent high-level monsters from being generated.  It is also true that
> keeping your XL low will reduce the probability of high-level monsters being
> generated.

Just as a followup, the number of entries in the eligibility table (weighted
by the generation frequency, though not accounting for alignment of the
levels) for the case with a minimum monster level of 8-11 (and a maximum
monster level of the max) is:

Min level     Entries
8             180
9             132
10            101
11            86

What this means is that an Archon (frequency 1) has a 1/180 chance of being
generated if the minimum monster level is 8, compared to 1/101 if the
minimum monster level is 10; a little less than twice as likely.

(I pulled this out of Dylan O'Donnell's mon2-343 spoiler with a quick
script.  Hopefully I did that right.)

So the moral of the story: if you're afraid of meeting Archons, it can't
hurt to try to keep your XL low.  But it's foolish to keep your XL low if
you are in need of the extra HP/MP, because it's always possible that the
tough enemies will get generated anyway.


